  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Compromise Accounts — Email Accounts", "technique": "T1586.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 08:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's Office365 module logged an authentication SUCCESS for alice.jones@corp.local from data.srcip=41.213.155.8 — an IP that geolocates to Egypt (ASN 36935 Telecom Egypt). The login used an OAuth2 password grant against /oauth2/v2.0/token on the IdP host 10.108.57.184, with scope 'openid Mail.ReadWrite offline_access'. There were NO preceding failed-login attempts for this account — the credential was not brute-forced, it was supplied correct on the first try, consistent with a previously stolen / purchased credential. alice.jones is a domestic employee whose historical sign-ins all originate from the corporate egress range; a successful password-grant from Egypt is a hard geo-velocity anomaly. Wazuh fired rule 5501 ('Authentication success from unusual geographic location').", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'office365' and 'authentication_success'; rule.description matched 'unusual geographic location' / 'login from new country' with data.srcip=41.213.155.8 against data.dstip=10.108.57.184. The univariate signature: a successful interactive auth for a domestic user from a never-before-seen foreign ASN with zero preceding failed attempts.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Email Collection — Inbox Rule manipulation", "technique": "T1564.008", "what_happened": "At 08:09:30 CEST (~7 minutes after the foreign login), the same data.srcip=41.213.155.8 created two inbox rules on alice.jones's mailbox via POST /api/v1/me/mailFolders/inbox/messageRules. Rule one ('Auto-organise') moves any message whose subject contains 're:' or 'fwd:' into the RSS Feeds folder and stops processing — this silently hides reply threads so the victim never sees responses to attacker-sent mail. Rule two ('Cleanup') permanently deletes any inbound message from security@corp.local, it-helpdesk@corp.local or siem-alerts@corp.local — this destroys the exact warning emails that would alert the victim or the SOC. Wazuh fired custom rule 91210 ('Inbox rule created: forward/delete pattern'). The attacker then issued a GET on the messageRules collection to confirm the rules were active.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=41.213.155.8 POSTed to /me/mailFolders/inbox/messageRules on data.dstip=10.108.57.184; rule.description matched 'inbox rule' / 'message rule created' and rule.id 91210 fired. The hide-replies + delete-IT/security-alerts pattern is the canonical defence-evasion inbox-rule signature (MITRE T1564.008 Hide Artifacts: Email Hiding Rules).", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:09:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection / Exfiltration — External email forwarding", "technique": "T1114.003", "what_happened": "At 08:18:05 CEST, the same data.srcip=41.213.155.8 issued PATCH /api/v1/me/mailboxSettings setting forwardingSmtpAddress to 'smtp:exfil-alice@protonmail.com' with forwardingEnabled=true — configuring silent server-side forwarding of all inbound mail to an attacker-controlled ProtonMail mailbox. The attacker then issued a GET on mailboxSettings to confirm forwarding was active. Wazuh fired custom rule 91212 ('External mailbox forwarding configured'). Combined with the step-2 inbox rules, this gives the attacker a durable, low-noise exfiltration channel: every message alice receives is copied out, and any reply or IT/security warning is hidden or destroyed.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=41.213.155.8 PATCHed /me/mailboxSettings on data.dstip=10.108.57.184 with a forwardingSmtpAddress pointing to an external free-mail provider; rule.description matched 'forwarding' / 'mailbox forwarding configured' and rule.id 91212 fired. External-forwarding-to-free-mail is the canonical email-collection exfiltration signature (MITRE T1114.003 Email Collection: Email Forwarding Rule).", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:18:05+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence — OAuth app consent grant", "technique": "T1098.001", "what_happened": "From 08:26:40 to 08:38:47 CEST, the same data.srcip=41.213.155.8 drove GET /common/oauth2/v2.0/authorize requests carrying client_id values for three never-before-seen third-party applications — '3d8b2e1a-ffff-0000-aaaa-c0mpromised01', 'DocumentReviewPro-xf9a2c7b1e43d' and 'MailSyncHelper-2026-fake' — each requesting the broad scope set 'Mail.Read Files.ReadWrite.All Contacts.ReadWrite offline_access'. The HTTP Referer was 'https://malicious-app-portal.io/grant' and the redirect_uri was 'https://attacker-callback.io/oauth'. The attacker then POSTed /common/oauth2/v2.0/token with grant_type=authorization_code to trade the consent for a long-lived offline_access token. Wazuh fired custom rule 91220 ('OAuth app consent granted by user'). This converts a session-bound credential compromise into durable, password-reset-surviving persistence.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=41.213.155.8 issued GET /common/oauth2/v2.0/authorize for multiple unknown client_id values with offline_access scopes, then POSTed /oauth2/v2.0/token with grant_type=authorization_code, all against data.dstip=10.108.57.184; rule.groups contained 'office365'/'oauth', rule.description matched 'OAuth app consent' / 'consent to application' and rule.id 91220 fired. The offline_access authorization_code grant for an unknown external app is the canonical OAuth-persistence signature (MITRE T1098.001 Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Credentials).", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:26:40+0200"}
  ],
